(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-spinal-cord-sensory-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Spinal Cord & Sensory Receptors — Anatomy Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between a sensation and a perception?","choices":["Sensation is the conscious interpretation of a stimulus; perception is simply the raw electrical signal traveling along the axon","Sensation is conscious awareness of a stimulus; perception is merely the raw arrival of information at the CNS","Sensation is sensory information arriving at the CNS; perception is the conscious awareness of that sensation","Sensation only occurs in the special senses; perception only occurs in the general senses, per this distinction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sensory information arriving at the CNS is called a sensation, while perception is the conscious awareness of a sensation. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the role of sensory receptors, per the material's introduction?","choices":["Structures used exclusively for motor output to effectors, not for detecting sensory input","Endocrine structures that produce and secrete hormones, entirely unrelated to the nervous system","The interface between the nervous system and the internal and external environment","Structures that only exist within the brain, with no peripheral location whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sensory receptors are the interface between the nervous system and the internal and external environment, picking up afferent (sensory) information that arrives at the CNS. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the difference between general senses and special senses?","choices":["General senses include vision, hearing, balance, smell, and taste, with receptors in complex organs; special senses include touch, pain, pressure, vibration, and temperature, with receptors distributed throughout the body","General senses (temperature, pain, touch, pressure, vibration, proprioception) have receptors distributed throughout the body; special senses (smell, taste, balance, hearing, vision) have receptors in complex sense organs","General senses include only pain and temperature receptors; special senses include only vision and hearing receptors, excluding all other sensory modalities entirely","General senses are located only in complex sense organs like the eyes and ears; special senses are distributed diffusely throughout the entire body"],"answer":1,"exp":"General senses (temperature, pain, touch, pressure, vibration, and proprioception) have receptors distributed throughout the body, while special senses (smell, taste, balance, hearing, and vision) have receptors located in complex sense organs like the eyes, ears, and taste buds. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is an example given of a nonspecific receptor, and a highly specific receptor?","choices":["Neither receptor type shows any specificity","Free nerve endings (respond to a variety of stimuli) versus retinal receptors (respond only to light)","Both free nerve endings and retinal receptors are equally nonspecific","Retinal receptors (respond to a variety of stimuli) versus free nerve endings (respond only to light)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Free nerve endings are the simplest receptors and respond to a variety of stimuli, while receptors of the retina are very specific and only respond to light. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does the identity of the active sensory neuron indicate, and what does the pattern of action potentials provide?","choices":["The identity indicates only the strength and duration of the stimulus; the pattern of action potentials indicates only its exact anatomical location","The identity indicates the location and nature of the stimulus; the pattern of action potentials (sensory coding) provides additional information like strength and duration","The identity indicates only whether the stimulus is painful or non-painful; the pattern conveys only the identity of the receptor type activated","The identity indicates the stimulus's exact intensity threshold; the pattern of action potentials indicates only the receptor's adaptation rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The identity of the active neuron indicates the location and nature of the stimulus, while the pattern of action potentials (sensory coding) provides additional information such as strength and duration. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the difference between exteroceptors, proprioceptors, and interoceptors, based on sensory location?","choices":["Exteroceptors provide information about body position; proprioceptors about pain only; interoceptors about the external environment","Exteroceptors provide information about body position; proprioceptors about the external environment; interoceptors about pain only","Exteroceptors provide information about the inside of the body; proprioceptors about the external environment; interoceptors about body position","Exteroceptors provide information about the external environment; proprioceptors about body position; interoceptors about the inside of the body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Based on sensory location, exteroceptors provide information about the external environment, proprioceptors provide information about the position of the body, and interoceptors provide information about the inside of the body. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between nociceptors, thermoreceptors, mechanoreceptors, and chemoreceptors, based on the nature of the stimulus?","choices":["Nociceptors respond to temperature changes; thermoreceptors to pain; mechanoreceptors to chemical composition of fluids; chemoreceptors to physical distortion of membranes","Nociceptors respond only to mechanical distortion; thermoreceptors to chemical composition; mechanoreceptors to pain; chemoreceptors to temperature changes","Only mechanoreceptors and chemoreceptors are classified this way; nociceptors and thermoreceptors are instead classified by receptor location","Nociceptors respond to pain; thermoreceptors to temperature changes; mechanoreceptors to physical distortion of membranes; chemoreceptors to chemical composition of body fluids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Classified by the nature of the stimulus: nociceptors respond to pain, thermoreceptors respond to temperature changes, mechanoreceptors are sensitive to physical distortion of cell membranes, and chemoreceptors monitor the chemical composition of body fluids. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between fast pain and slow pain?","choices":["Fast pain (\"burning pain\") reaches the CNS slowly, associated with deep cuts; slow pain (\"prickling pain\") reaches the CNS quickly, with immediate onset","Fast pain (\"prickling pain\") reaches the CNS quickly, associated with deep cuts; slow pain (\"burning pain\") reaches the CNS slowly, with delayed onset and longer duration","Fast pain and slow pain both describe distinct categories of thermoreceptor-mediated temperature sensation, not true nociceptive pain","Both fast and slow pain are relayed exclusively by the dorsal columns at identical conduction speeds, bypassing the spinothalamic tract"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fast pain (\"prickling pain\") reaches the CNS quickly and is associated with deep cuts or similar injuries, while slow pain (\"burning pain\") reaches the CNS slowly, has delayed onset, and lasts longer, even for the same type of injury. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is referred pain?","choices":["Pain that only occurs in skeletal muscle following direct trauma, never originating from visceral organs","Painful sensation from visceral organs that is perceived as coming from another region, with an unknown exact mechanism","A type of thermoreceptor sensation triggered by extreme temperature changes in deep visceral tissue","Pain that is always perceived precisely at the exact visceral organ where the injury occurred"],"answer":1,"exp":"Referred pain is a painful sensation from visceral organs that is perceived as coming from another region; the exact mechanism is unknown. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where are thermoreceptors found, and what type of receptor are they (in terms of adaptation)?","choices":["Found in the dermis, skeletal muscles, liver, and hypothalamus, as free nerve endings; phasic receptors that adapt quickly to a stable temperature","Found in bone tissue and cartilage exclusively, as encapsulated endings; tonic receptors that never adapt to a stable temperature","Found only in the retina and cornea, as specialized discs; phasic receptors that adapt quickly to bright light","Found only in the epidermis and hair follicles; tonic receptors that never adapt to a stable temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thermoreceptors are found in the dermis, skeletal muscles, liver, and hypothalamus, existing as free nerve endings; they are phasic receptors, very active when temperature changes but quickly adapting to a stable temperature. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Are cold receptors or hot receptors more numerous, per the material?","choices":["Hot receptors are more numerous than cold receptors","Cold receptors are more numerous than hot receptors","Neither cold nor hot receptors exist as a distinct category","Cold and hot receptors are exactly equal in number"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cold receptors are more numerous than hot receptors. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are the three types of mechanoreceptors described in the material?","choices":["Tactile receptors, baroreceptors, and proprioceptors","Only tactile receptors exist as a mechanoreceptor type","Photoreceptors, chemoreceptors, and thermoreceptors","Nociceptors, thermoreceptors, and chemoreceptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three types of mechanoreceptors are tactile receptors, baroreceptors, and proprioceptors. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the difference between fine touch/pressure receptors and crude touch/pressure receptors (tactile receptor subtypes)?","choices":["Fine touch receptors monitor internal organ stretch and blood pressure; crude touch receptors monitor only joint angle and tendon tension changes","Fine and crude touch receptors are simply alternate names for baroreceptors that monitor organ stretch due to pressure","Fine touch/pressure receptors provide only generalized information about location; crude touch/pressure receptors provide detailed shape and texture information","Fine touch/pressure receptors provide detailed information about location, shape, size, and texture; crude touch/pressure receptors provide generalized information about stimulus location"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fine touch and pressure receptors provide detailed information about the location, shape, size, and texture of a stimulus, while crude touch and pressure receptors provide generalized information about stimulus location. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What are baroreceptors, and where are they located?","choices":["Receptors that monitor joint position and tension changes, located only in the tendons and joint capsules of the limbs","Stretch receptors that monitor changes in organ stretch due to pressure, located in structures like the stomach, small intestine, urinary bladder, carotid artery, lungs, and large intestine","Receptors that detect light touch and vibration, located only in the epidermis and dermis of the skin","Chemoreceptors that monitor blood chemical composition and oxygen levels, located in the carotid and aortic bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Baroreceptors are stretch receptors that monitor changes in the stretch of organs due to pressure, located in structures like the stomach, small intestine, urinary bladder, carotid artery, lungs, and large intestine. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What do proprioceptors monitor, and what are Golgi tendon organs versus muscle spindles?","choices":["Proprioceptors monitor skin temperature and stretch; Golgi tendon organs monitor muscle fiber length, while muscle spindles monitor tendon tension","Proprioceptors monitor only skin stretch and vibration; Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles are actually the same structure with different names","Proprioceptors monitor blood pressure and organ stretch; Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles are both located exclusively in ligaments","Proprioceptors monitor joint position and tension; Golgi tendon organs are receptors in tendons monitoring tension, while muscle spindles are receptors in muscles monitoring fiber length"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proprioceptors monitor the position of joints and tension in tendons/ligaments; Golgi tendon organs are the receptors in tendons that monitor tension, while muscle spindles are receptors in the muscles that monitor the length of muscle fibers upon contraction. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Where are chemoreceptors found, per the material?","choices":["Only in the retina and cornea, where they respond to changes in ambient light and pH","Only in the skin's epidermis and hair follicles, responding to sweat gland chemical secretions","Respiratory centers of the medulla oblongata, carotid arteries (carotid bodies), and the aortic arch (aortic bodies)","Only in the cerebellum and pons, monitoring cerebrospinal fluid glucose and pH levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chemoreceptors, which detect small changes in the concentration of chemicals and respond to water-soluble or lipid-soluble compounds, are found in the respiratory centers of the medulla oblongata, the carotid arteries (carotid bodies), and the aortic arch (aortic bodies). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What sensations do Merkel discs detect, and which tract do they relay through?","choices":["Deep touch and high-frequency vibration; relay through the dorsal columns","Light touch; relay through the ventral spinothalamic tract","Pain; relay through Lissauer's tract","Pressure, heat, and skin stretching; relay through the dorsal columns"],"answer":1,"exp":"Merkel discs detect light touch and relay this information through the ventral spinothalamic tract. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What sensations do Meissner's corpuscles detect, and which pathway do they relay through?","choices":["Pressure and skin stretching; relay through the ventral spinothalamic tract pathway","Pain and temperature sensation only; relay through the lateral spinothalamic tract","Light touch, movement, and low-frequency vibration; relay through the dorsal columns","Deep touch and high-frequency vibration; relay through the spinothalamic tract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Meissner's corpuscles detect light touch, movement, and low-frequency vibration, relaying this information through the dorsal columns. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What do Ruffini corpuscles detect, and how is their heat sensation relayed differently from their pressure/stretch sensation?","choices":["Detect light touch and movement only; relays exclusively through the ventral spinothalamic tract pathway","Detect deep touch and high-frequency vibration only; all sensations relay exclusively through the dorsal columns","Detect only pain and temperature; all sensations relay exclusively through the spinothalamic tract","Detect pressure, heat, and skin stretching; pressure/stretch relays through the dorsal columns, but heat instead goes to the spinothalamic tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ruffini corpuscles detect pressure, heat, and skin stretching; pressure and stretch sensations relay through the dorsal columns, but heat sensation instead goes to the spinothalamic tract. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What do Pacinian corpuscles detect, and which pathway relays this information?","choices":["Pain; relay through the ventral spinothalamic tract","Pressure and heat; relay through Lissauer's tract","Light touch and low-frequency vibration; relay through the spinothalamic tract","Deep touch and high-frequency vibrations; relay through the dorsal columns"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pacinian corpuscles detect deep touch and high-frequency vibrations, relaying this information through the dorsal columns. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What do free nerve endings function as, and which tract relays their information?","choices":["Function as nociceptors; relay through the spinothalamic tracts","Function as chemoreceptors; relay through the medial lemniscus only","Function as proprioceptors; relay through the corticospinal tract","Function as baroreceptors; relay through the dorsal columns"],"answer":0,"exp":"Free nerve endings function as nociceptors, relaying pain information through the spinothalamic tracts. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What structures make up the olfactory epithelium, per the material?","choices":["Taste buds, gustatory epithelial cells, and supporting cells of the tongue","Olfactory sensory neurons, supporting cells, and basal epithelial cells","Only olfactory sensory neurons, with no supporting or basal cells","Photoreceptor cells, bipolar cells, and ganglion cells of the retina"],"answer":1,"exp":"The olfactory epithelium contains olfactory sensory neurons, supporting cells, and basal epithelial cells. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What does the lamina propria underlying the olfactory epithelium contain?","choices":["Olfactory glands (producing mucus), blood vessels, and nerves","Taste buds and gustatory epithelial supporting cells","Only bone tissue and cartilage, with no glandular structures","Photoreceptors and retinal pigment epithelium cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lamina propria, an underlying loose connective tissue, contains olfactory glands (which produce mucus), blood vessels, and nerves. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What are olfactory sensory neurons, structurally, and how are they replaced?","choices":["Highly modified neurons with an apical end containing multiple cilia projecting into the nasal cavity; replaced regularly by regenerative basal cells","Highly modified neurons found only in the tongue, replaced regularly by taste bud regeneration every ten days","Glial-like support cells, not true neurons, replaced continuously by dividing microglia in the epithelium","Typical unmodified neurons with a single dendrite, never replaced once formed during development"],"answer":0,"exp":"Olfactory sensory neurons are highly modified neurons with an apical end containing multiple cilia that project beyond the epithelium into the nasal cavity (covered by mucus from olfactory glands); receptor cells are replaced regularly by regenerative basal cells in the olfactory epithelium. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What cranial nerve do olfactory receptor axons form, and through what foramina do they leave the epithelium?","choices":["Form CN VII, leaving through the stylomastoid foramen to innervate facial muscles","Form CN II, leaving through the optic canal to synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus","Form CN X, leaving through the jugular foramen to innervate thoracic and abdominal organs","Form CN I, leaving through the cribriform foramina to synapse in the olfactory bulb"],"answer":3,"exp":"Axons of olfactory receptors leave the olfactory epithelium through the cribriform foramina and synapse on neurons in the olfactory bulb; these axons form CN I (the olfactory nerve). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"After the olfactory bulb, where do olfactory impulses travel, and where do they ultimately arrive?","choices":["Travel via the optic tract, arriving only at the primary visual cortex of the occipital lobe","Travel via the spinothalamic tract, arriving at the primary sensory cortex only","Travel via the corticospinal tract, arriving at motor neurons of the spinal cord","Travel via the olfactory tract, arriving at the cerebral cortex, hypothalamus, and limbic system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Impulses travel from the olfactory bulb to the brain via the olfactory tract, arriving at the cerebral cortex, hypothalamus, and limbic system. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where are gustatory receptors found in adults, and what are the four types of lingual papillae?","choices":["Found only in the pharynx and soft palate; only two types of taste papillae exist there","Found in the olfactory epithelium of the nasal cavity; three types of papillae exist there","Found in papillae on the dorsal surface of the tongue; the four types are filiform, fungiform, foliate, and vallate","Found in the ear; the four types are identical to lingual papillae types"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gustatory receptors are found in papillae on the dorsal surface of the tongue in adults, and there are four types of lingual papillae: filiform, fungiform, foliate, and vallate. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What do gustatory epithelial cells (taste receptors) have that extends through the taste pore, and what stimulates them?","choices":["Stereocilia that detect sound vibrations by bending in response to endolymph fluid movement within the cochlea","Photoreceptive discs stacked within the outer segment; light striking the discs stimulates the cell","Slender microvilli (\"taste hairs\") that extend through the taste pore into surrounding fluid; dissolved chemicals contacting the microvilli stimulate the cell","Cilia that extend into the nasal cavity mucus layer; airborne odorant molecules stimulate the cell directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each gustatory cell has slender microvilli (also called \"taste hairs\") that extend through the taste pore into the surrounding fluid; dissolved chemicals contact the microvilli and stimulate the gustatory cell. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What cranial nerves carry gustatory impulses, and where do first-order synapses occur?","choices":["CN II and III; first-order synapses occur directly in the thalamus, bypassing the medulla","CN I only; first-order synapses occur within the olfactory bulb itself","CN V and VI; first-order synapses occur in the cerebellar cortex","CN VII, IX, and X; first-order synapses occur in the nucleus solitarius of the medulla oblongata"],"answer":3,"exp":"Gustatory impulses travel through CN VII, IX, and X, with first-order synapses occurring in the nucleus solitarius of the medulla oblongata, before second-order synapses in the thalamus and information arriving at the gustatory cortex. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What are the four (possibly six) primary taste sensations listed in the material?","choices":["Only sweet and salty, with no other primary sensations described","Sweet, salty, sour, bitter (plus umami and water)","Sweet, sour, spicy, and bitter (plus minty and metallic)","Umami, spicy, minty, and sour (plus salty and metallic)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The primary taste sensations are sweet, salty, sour, and bitter, with umami (characteristic of beef and chicken broth) and water receptors (mainly in the pharynx) as possible additional sensations, for a total of four to six. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
